INSERT INTO alert_explanations VALUES(38,replace('**What Happened**\nA critical alert was triggered by the malware scanner after detecting a PHP backdoor file named "backdoor.php" in the directory "/var/www/uploads/". The file contains suspicious code that uses base64_decode and system() functions, which are common techniques for executing arbitrary commands or maintaining unauthorized access. This indicates a potential compromise where an attacker may have uploaded malicious code to gain control over the system.\n\n**Why It Matters**\nThis is a critical severity event because PHP backdoors allow attackers to execute commands, exfiltrate data, or pivot to other systems. The presence of base64_decode and system() aligns with MITRE ATT&CK techniques T1059 (Command and Scripting Interpreter) and T1071 (Application Layer Protocol), placing this in the "Execution" and "Persistence" stages of the kill chain. If unaddressed, this could lead to full system compromise, data theft, or lateral movement within the network.\n\n**Recommended Actions**\n1. Immediately isolate the affected server from the network to prevent further spread or data exfiltration.\n2. Preserve the backdoor.php file and its logs as forensic evidence before any cleanup or deletion.\n3. Conduct a full system scan for additional malware or persistence mechanisms across all web directories and user accounts.\n4. Review access logs for "/var/www/uploads/" to identify the source of the upload and any related suspicious activity.\n\n⚠️ This explanation may contain inferred details - verify against raw alert data.','\n',char(10)),'mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603','2026-07-05 17:16:28');
INSERT INTO alert_explanations VALUES(49,replace('**What Happened**\n\nA critical security alert was triggered by the correlation engine, indicating a three-stage attack involving a PHP exploit chain. The IP address 45.33.32.156 was detected in a sequence of malicious activities, including a path traversal attempt on the "/files" endpoint and an exfiltration event. The attack follows a known pattern where an attacker exploits a vulnerability to upload a webshell, spawns a malicious process, and establishes outbound command-and-control (C2) communication.\n\n**Why It Matters**\n\nThis attack represents a high-severity threat with potential for full system compromise. The MITRE ATT&CK mapping includes T1505.003 (Web Shell), T1055 (Process Injection), and T1005 (Data from Local System) for exfiltration. The kill chain stage aligns with actions on objectives, where the attacker has likely gained persistence and is attempting to move laterally or extract sensitive data. Immediate action is required to prevent data loss or further compromise.\n\n**Recommended Actions**\n\n1. Immediately block the IP address 45.33.32.156 at the firewall and WAF to prevent further communication.\n2. Isolate any systems that may have been accessed by this IP, especially those involved in the path traversal attempt or exfiltration.\n3. Review logs for additional indicators of compromise, focusing on the time window around the detected events.\n4. Conduct a forensic analysis of affected systems to determine the extent of the breach and identify any persistent backdoors.\n\n⚠️ This explanation may contain inferred details - verify against raw alert data.','\n',char(10)),'mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603','2026-07-03 16:33:49');
